.TH CAT 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
cat \- concatenate and print files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cat
.RB [ \-u ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cat
reads
.I file
in sequence and writes their contents to standard output in the same
sequence, byte for byte, with no line-ending translation of any kind.
.PP
If no
.I file
operand is given, standard input is used. If a
.I file
is
.BR \- ,
.B cat
reads from standard input at that point in the sequence \(em so
.B \-
is not merely a synonym for "no operands"; it can appear anywhere in
a mixed operand list and is honored positionally.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-u
Write bytes to standard output without delay as each is read. Accepted
as a real no-op: this implementation already writes every block it
reads immediately with no additional buffering, so there is nothing
for
.B \-u
to disable.
.B \-u
is only recognized while it appears before every
.I file
operand; once the first non-\c
.B \-u
argument is seen, option scanning stops, and a later
.B \-u
in the argument list is treated as a filename operand instead.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file. If
.B \-
is given, standard input is used at that point in the sequence.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input files were output successfully.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred; processing continues with the next operand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR echo (1),
.BR tee (1),
.BR head (1)
